# NOTE: this file must be UTF-8 (it is deliberately pure ASCII). Git parses
# .gitattributes as UTF-8 bytes and silently ignores a UTF-16 one -- which is
# what `... > .gitattributes` produces in Windows PowerShell 5.1. This file was
# UTF-16LE until 2026-08-03, so none of its rules applied.
# Verify with: git check-attr -a <some-file>

# Normalize line endings: LF in the repository and in the working tree, so the
# ruff formatter (line-ending = "lf" in ruff.toml) and Windows checkouts stop
# rewriting each other's files.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Windows scripts keep CRLF -- .bat/.cmd in particular can misbehave with LF.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.ps1 text eol=crlf

# Shell scripts must keep LF so they run on the Linux hosts (already implied by
# the default above; stated explicitly so it survives changes to that rule).
*.sh text eol=lf

# Vendored third-party trees: leave the bytes exactly as they were committed.
#
# These arrived with CRLF and are tracked, so the `* text=auto eol=lf` rule above makes
# git report all 3381 of them as modified for ever -- the files on disk are byte-identical
# to the index, but git converts the worktree copy to LF before comparing. `git add -A`
# then sweeps hundreds of lines of pure line-ending churn into unrelated commits.
#
# `-text` stops the conversion in both directions. Nothing is rewritten and nothing is
# ignored: these are still tracked, because units need them at runtime.
#
# Note `.gitignore` also lists `src/Standa/`, which has never had any effect -- gitignore
# does not apply to files that are already tracked.
src/Standa/** -text
